 Add prefix drill-down, reliable AS organization enrichment and configurable anomaly thresholds, then benchmark higher client counts. Before public deployment, add authentication, TLS and deployment limits. No organization names are inferred or invented. Historical storage, exhaustive route state and validated incident attribution remain outside this in-memory version.
 
 Protocol reference: [RIPE RIS Live manual](https://ris-live.ripe.net/manual/).
+
+## Routing forecasting comparison
+
+The research question is: **Can two regression models predict next-minute observed BGP UPDATE volume better than repeating the previous minute?** This is forecasting collector observations, not detecting attacks, predicting global outages, or claiming Internet-wide visibility.
+
+The backend automatically captures genuine, global, receipt-time minute aggregates in `data/forecast.sqlite3` (SQLite WAL). No prefixes or raw events are persisted by this feature. It retains 14 days, reads at most 4,326 recent rows for modelling, and retains the latest 20 evaluation reports. Startup partial minutes, observed upstream disconnections, missed sampling intervals, and missing minutes are excluded. A connected quiet minute is a valid zero. Five contiguous valid past minutes form each input and the following complete minute supplies its label. Dashboard scope filters do not affect this global training stream.
+
+Both **RandomForestRegressor** and **HistGradientBoostingRegressor**, from scikit-learn 1.7.2, solve the identical regression problem. Inputs are five lagged minute UPDATE counts, their mean/std/min/max and latest change, latest withdrawal share, average AS path length, flap count, collector count, announcements and withdrawals. These are derived from existing aggregate fields; routing/event semantics are unchanged. Each report records exact feature names, fixed parameters, dependency versions, dataset SHA-256, sample counts, time ranges, and model version.
+
+At least **180 labelled examples** are required: approximately **3 hours 6 minutes** of uninterrupted genuine observations from a fresh start, depending on alignment and connection health. This is a practical collection threshold, not proof of statistical adequacy. The dashboard shows progress while collecting. It never displays fixture-trained scores. History survives backend restarts; gaps still invalidate the affected five-minute contexts.
+
+Evaluation uses the first 70% of examples for training and the later 30% for holdout, with a one-minute embargo between the final training label end and first holdout origin. The two models and repeat-previous-minute baseline use identical examples. There is no shuffling, hyperparameter search, random early-stopping split, or claim that model disagreement is a confidence interval. Metrics are **MAE and RMSE in UPDATE counts per 60-second window**, evaluation fit time, measured batch prediction milliseconds per row, and serialized model size. Baseline timing/size is not benchmarked. The report identifies lowest holdout MAE even when the baseline wins. Model-size measurement uses serialization only; no model files are loaded or deserialized.
+
+After evaluation, both live models are refit on all currently available past labelled examples. Initial training and hourly refresh run in one separate process with one computational thread; SQLite operations run outside the event loop. Minute sampling is independent of model jobs, and ingestion contains no model or database calls. API responses are cached in memory and fetched by an independent five-second frontend poll, not attached to every WebSocket snapshot. There is at most one training job and one prospective prediction job. A missed deadline skips that forecast; it never backfills a prediction using later observations.
+
+Prospective predictions are generated within the first five seconds of aligned minute `[t, t+60)`, using aggregates ending at `t`; actual generation time is recorded. Thus remaining lead time is approximately 55–60 seconds, not always a full 60 seconds from UI display. Predictions are stored before target completion and matched to actuals when a valid target minute matures. Incomplete targets are excluded. The UI reports prospective MAE for up to 60 matched predictions of the current model version (within its latest 120 captured forecasts), separately from the chronological holdout. Failed storage/training affects this feature only; live routing continues.
+
+Run from the repository root:
+
+```powershell
+.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m unittest discover -s backend -p "test_*.py" -v
+.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m backend.forecast_cli export --output captured-minutes.csv
+.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m backend.forecast_cli evaluate --output evaluation.json
+```
+
+CLI exports and comparisons use the same recent bounded capture window and feature implementation. An evaluation before enough history exists exits with a collection message and no scores. `/api/forecast` exposes collection state and comparisons; `/api/forecast/report` returns the latest report, or HTTP 409 during cold start. A retained report is identified as the last evaluation while models are warming up after restart. Keep a copy of the exported aggregates together with the report for exact experiment reproduction before retention removes them. Never import fixture data into the production capture database.
+
+Limitations: a single chronological holdout is not cross-validation or proof of generalization; neighboring windows have correlated inputs; several days and different routing conditions are preferable for a final academic comparison. Collector coverage and connection changes can affect counts. Availability is checked at sampling resolution (0.5 s), so an outage entirely between observations can escape detection. A stalled upstream that the backend still considers connected cannot be distinguished from genuine quiet activity by this layer. Forecast jobs may skip windows during refresh or worker recovery. This implementation introduces local SQLite and the scikit-learn backend dependency, but no new frontend dependency, simulated observations, or dashboard chart types.
+
+Method references: [scikit-learn time-series lagged-feature evaluation](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/auto_examples/applications/plot_time_series_lagged_features.html), [Random Forest regressor](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestRegressor.html), [Histogram Gradient Boosting regressor](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.ensemble.HistGradientBoostingRegressor.html).
